# Logitech G502 LIGHTSPEED (046d:407f through its 046d:c539 receiver or a
# POWERPLAY mat's 046d:c53a, 046d:c08d cabled) -> uaccess on its own hidraw
# node through the receiver, and on its HID++ interface, 02, on its cable.
#
# Not yet checked on this hardware. Its receiver is a LIGHTSPEED one that the
# kernel's hid-logitech-dj binds as it binds the G305's, so the mouse gets a
# hidraw node of its own, matched by its HID device name as 70-g305.rules
# matches the G305's (Solaar issue 2825's log: 0003:046D:407F.0008 under the
# c539's interface 02). A property match would also take in the receiver's
# interfaces. The c539 pairs one device (Solaar's listing: "a maximum of
# 1"), so no keyboard can share its node; on a POWERPLAY's c53a the other
# slot is the mat's own chip, which this rule does not grant.
#
# The node still carries the keystrokes the mouse sends for a button its
# onboard profile binds to a key, as 70-g502x.rules says of the G502 X PLUS's.
#
# Cabled, no source names the c08d's HID++ interface: 02 is assumed, the
# interface the PRO WIRELESS's and the G903 LIGHTSPEED HERO's firmware give
# their vendor collection. Interface 01 is not granted.
#
# No event node is granted: in host mode Gearbench reads the buttons it binds
# through HID++ on the hidraw nodes below, and outside host mode none.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0003:046D:407F.*", TAG+="uaccess"
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c08d", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="02", TAG+="uaccess"
